-- title:
--   Deuring: supersingular endomorphism ring is a maximal order
-- statement:
--   Let $k$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic a prime $p$ which is algebraic over $\mathbb{F}_p = \mathrm{ZMod}\ p$, and let $X$ be a Weierstrass curve over $k$ which is elliptic. Assume $X$ is supersingular in the sense that every point $P$ of the affine point group of $X$ with $p \cdot P = 0$ is already $0$. Let $a, b \in \mathbb{Q}$ satisfy [`QuaternionAlgebra.IsDefiniteRamifiedExactlyAt a b p`](def/QuaternionAlgebra_EichlerOrder.html#L87), i.e. $a < 0$, $b < 0$, and for every height-one prime $v$ of the ring of integers of $\mathbb{Q}$ the base change $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b] \otimes_{\mathbb{Q}} \mathbb{Q}_v$ is a division algebra (every nonzero element is a unit) precisely when $p$ lies in $v$. The conclusion asserts the existence of a $\mathbb{Z}$-submodule $\Lambda$ of $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ which is a maximal order, meaning that $\Lambda$ contains $1$, is closed under multiplication, spans $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ over $\mathbb{Q}$ and is finitely generated, and that every such $\Lambda'$ containing $\Lambda$ equals $\Lambda$, together with an injective ring homomorphism $\theta$ from [`WeierstrassCurve.rationalEndSubring k X`](def/WeierstrassCurve_RationalEnd.html#L32) — the subring of the additive endomorphism monoid of the points of the base change of $X$ to $k$ generated by those $\alpha$ that are either $0$ or rationally represented — whose range is exactly $\Lambda$ as a subset.
--
--   This is Deuring's theorem that the endomorphism ring of a supersingular elliptic curve over an algebraically closed field algebraic over $\mathbb{F}_p$ is a maximal order in the definite quaternion algebra over $\mathbb{Q}$ ramified exactly at $p$ and $\infty$, here in the form where the quaternion algebra $\mathbb{H}[\mathbb{Q}, a, b]$ is given in advance rather than produced by the statement. It feeds the companion result [`WeierstrassCurve.exists_supersingular_rationalEndSubring_range_eq_of_isMaximalOrder`](thm.html#WeierstrassCurve.exists_supersingular_rationalEndSubring_range_eq_of_isMaximalOrder), which runs the correspondence in the opposite direction.
